From b385be3937d624c552ed3c373598af1dc1fd1c11 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BreDoyliesSea <doylies@encrypt.eu>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 06:51:44 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 49/52] packages: validate param.sfo and read the last key
 correctly (#4204)

* packages: Read a param.sfo key only up to its null terminator

A key's length was taken from the next key's offset, and for the last key
from the start of the data table. The key table is padded to four bytes, so
the last key kept the padding as trailing nulls and never matched a lookup:
VERSION in most games (two to three bytes of padding in Spelunky and
Disgaea 4), and TITLE_ID in a param.sfo where it is the last key. An install
then read an empty title ID and its destination became ux0/app itself, which
an overwrite deletes (#4113).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Refuse a param.sfo whose tables do not fit the file

sfo::load trusted the header, so an encrypted param.sfo - UPPERS (PCSG00633)
ships its save data template as one, at disc/savedata/sce_sys/param.sfo - was
read as a header claiming three billion entries: installing the game aborted
with std::bad_alloc, and an install that got past it read no title ID (#4113).
The magic and every table offset are now checked against the file, and the
installers skip a param.sfo that does not load instead of reusing the
previous one's details.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Start each param.sfo read from empty app info

The installers read every param.sfo into the same emuenv.app_info. A key
the file lacks kept the value the previous install left there, so a
param.sfo without TITLE_ID got the last app's title ID, passed the
empty title ID check, and a folder install then removed that app's
directory and copied the new content into it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Refuse an empty null-terminated param.sfo entry

A UTF8_NULL entry of length 0 has no null byte to drop, and reading it
took the end of an empty buffer minus one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Add the key table bound in 64 bits

The key table start plus a key offset was added in 32 bits, so a
param.sfo with a key table near 4 GB could wrap past the bound check.
The data check below already adds in 64 bits.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Refuse a pkg or vci whose param.sfo has no usable ID

The pkg and vci installers ignored whether the param.sfo was readable,
and built the destination from its title ID before removing whatever was
there. With app_info now starting empty, an unreadable param.sfo or one
without TITLE_ID left an empty title ID, so the destination was ux0/app
itself and the install removed every installed app.

Both now refuse such a package: an unreadable param.sfo, an empty title
ID (content ID for a theme), or a DLC content ID too short to take its
label from.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Read a null-terminated param.sfo value up to its terminator

A UTF8_NULL value dropped its last byte whether or not it was the
terminator, so a value written without one lost its last character: a
TITLE_ID of PCSE00001 became PCSE0000. It now ends at the first null
byte, like the keys do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* packages: Remove the VCI staging folder when its param.sfo is rejected

The new param.sfo check returned without removing VCI_TMP, which holds
the carved gro0 image and the extracted app, as the later failures do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

